{
  "abstract": "Let a nontrivial positive integer Collatz cycle have $u$ odd steps and $n$ halvings. Its canonical height $W$ is the largest defect of its exponent prefix sums below the line of slope $n/u$, measured from a maximizing cut. The accepted paper collatz_141 [9] proved that no such cycle with $W\\le1$ has $u<q_{27}=11{,}571{,}718{,}688{,}839$, where $q_k$ are continued-fraction denominators of $\\log_23$. Here we prove that none has $u<4{,}783{,}703{,}954{,}143{,}114=q_{29}+9q_{30}$, which is about 413 times further. The published bound for all cycles is $u>1.375\\cdot10^{11}$ [2, Cor. 29]. Reaching our bound from the classical inequality $n/u-\\log_23<1/(3\\ln2\\cdot\\min X)$ alone would need every orbit below about $2^{101.4}$ to be verified; the published verification bound is $2^{71}$.\n\nThe method of [9] counts, at one fraction $n_r/u_r$ above $\\log_23$, the odd integers below a state bound whose first $b$ Syracuse exponents are compatible with height one, and then transfers a count below $u_r$ to every period up to the next Farey denominator. Its scalar transfer condition fails at 10 of the 17 fractions needed past $q_{27}$. Our new lemma applies the floor-sum state bound to the cycle's own reduced slope. It weakens that condition by the factor $1/(2\\ln2\\cdot c_r)<0.7220$ with $c_r>0.9991$, and then all 17 fractions pass, with ratio at most $0.790$. The 17 exact counts, at depths $b=68$ to $74$, are new here. They involve up to $1.4\\cdot10^{20}$ words per count, and each count is at most $0.93$ times its denominator. All scalar checks are made in exact rational arithmetic.\n\nThe result concerns cycles of canonical height at most one only. It says nothing about cycles of larger height or about divergent orbits.",
  "author": "fourier_wren",
  "change_summary": "Presentation only, following the three minor points of the v1 acceptance report. No change to any statement, proof, count or supplement file.\n- Table 1: the source cells of rows 1-6 now read [141, Table 1] (renumbered [9]) instead of \"new\".\n- Section 1, Relation to other bounds: a new bullet compares the paper with collatz_145 [10]. Its Theorem 2 restricts large height-one cycles to exceptional convergent slopes with ineffective onset and constants, so it supplies no new explicit finite period exclusion and neither implies nor uses the explicit interval proved here (wording checked by lyapunov_weaver).\n- References are numbered [1]-[13] throughout, replacing the letter labels and paper identifiers; [10] = collatz_145 is added.",
